# ============================================================
.worktrees/
.superpowers/

# ============================================================
# Assay-hygiene working dir. extract/ carries a full dump of
# samples.json_metadata pulled from seek_production (163k rows,
# 260 MB of real sample metadata) — never commit it.
# ============================================================
assay-hygiene/
# ...and every sibling copy of it. `assay-hygiene-bak/` held 195 MB of the same
# real sample metadata while matching NO pattern here, one `git add -A` away from
# a PUBLIC repo -- and this repo already needed a history rewrite on 2026-08-21 to
# strip 1,570 sample identifiers. A prefix glob, so a future `-bak2` or `-old` is
# covered by construction rather than by someone remembering to add a line.
assay-hygiene-*/

# ============================================================
# The operator's rulings, copied into tests/fixtures/ so the
# tests that read them can run. THEY ARE CURATION OUTPUT AND
# MUST NEVER BE COMMITTED: `mode2-rulings.tsv` carries protocol
# and specimen strings from the same namespace the 2026-08-21
# history rewrite had to strip 1,570 identifiers from, and this
# repository is PUBLIC. The tests skip when the files are
# absent, so a fresh clone is green without them -- which is
# exactly why nothing here may rely on someone remembering.
#
# UNANCHORED, MATCHING AT ANY DEPTH, and that is not tidiness.
# `mode2-rulings-backup-2026-08-20.tsv` -- a byte-identical copy
# of the whole ruling file -- was sitting UNTRACKED AND
# UNIGNORED in the repository root on 2026-08-24, one `git add
# -A` from being public. A rule scoped to `tests/fixtures/`
# would have left it there. The globs are on the NAME, so the
# next copy is covered wherever someone drops it and whatever
# suffix they add.
# ============================================================
*rulings*.tsv
*verdicts*.csv
